{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '230,270p' skills/mission/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"Each task in tasks.md must include topology metadata in this fixed format:\n\n```markdown\n- [ ] Task N: <name>\n  Depends on: <prior task numbers, or \"none\">\n  Consumes: <surfaces from prior tasks -- types, interfaces, endpoints>\n  Produces: <surfaces this task creates -- types, modules, interfaces, endpoints>\n  Layers: <logical architectural layers touched, e.g. domain, transport, storage>\n```\n\nLayers are logical groupings of modules (domain, transport, storage, configuration, etc.) defined in the design narrative, not columns from the Module Inventory table.\n\nWhen the approved strategy (Phase 4.3a) is layered or hybrid and a task's Layers field lists more than 2 architectural layers, flag the task for human review with a recommendation to split or approve as-is. When the approved strategy is vertical, the layer count flag does not apply since vertical tasks are expected to cross all layers.\n\nWhen a task adds public surface area to a module (new exports, API endpoints, CLI flags), include the implementation behind that surface in the same task. Do not split a module's interface from its implementation across tasks -- the per-task validator judges depth from a single diff and will flag an interface without the work behind it.\n\n#### Mandatory Closing Tasks\n\nEvery mission's tasks.md MUST include these closing tasks after all feature tasks:\n\n1. **Smoke test (mandatory).** Write or update one end-to-end test that runs the project's primary user flow against real dependencies. No project-internal mocks. Place it where the project's standard test command runs it. If the mission produces no runnable user flow, note that in the handoff.\n\n2. **Cleanup task (mandatory).** Remove unused dependencies, dead code, leftover scaffolding, temporary artifacts, and any backwards-compatibility shims. Workers sometimes leave debris that per-task validation does not catch. This task cleans up the codebase before the shape re-check so the re-check does not flag soon-to-be-deleted surface area.\n\n3. **Module shape re-check (mandatory for missions with 5+ feature tasks; optional otherwise).** Re-read design.md's module descriptions. For each module, compare the description to what the code actually exposes -- in whatever form is natural for the project (public functions, React props, HTTP endpoints, CLI flags, exported types, data schemas, message topics, job parameters, config keys, whatever contract callers actually depend on). If reality has drifted meaningfully from the description, either update design.md to match (deliberate change) or refactor the code back (unintended drift). \"Meaningful drift\" means: any public surface item added, removed, or renamed that is not reflected in design.md; any change to the vocabulary or contract callers depend on; or a cumulative widening where a module that was described as having N operations now has noticeably more. Temporary shims and internal helpers do not count. For small missions (under 5 feature tasks) a one-line \"no drift observed\" note in the handoff is sufficient -- this step exists to catch cumulative drift across many tasks, which rarely occurs in short missions. This catches drift that per-task scrutiny cannot see.\n\n   Write the findings to `mission/validations/module-shape-audit.md` -- a short free-form note listing each module, whether drift was found, and what was done about it (\"no drift,\" \"design.md updated,\" or \"refactored\"). No required format; the file exists as a record that the check happened.\n\n4. **Documentation task (mandatory).** Write or update documentation for what was built. The orchestrator recommends the documentation scope based on what the feature tasks produce -- this varies by mission type. Not every mission needs install instructions, architecture diagrams, or API docs. Use terms from `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` consistently. Any commands shown in docs must be executed against the built artifacts and the actual output pasted -- don't show output that wasn't produced. If unsure what documentation is needed, ask the human during this review.\n\n5. **CI configuration task (optional).** Add or update CI/CD pipeline configuration. The orchestrator recommends whether to include this based on the change scope. The human decides during this review.\n\nIf the design (Phase 4.3) identified refactoring needed for existing code, those tasks go before the feature tasks in tasks.md.\n\nPresent the full task list (including closing tasks) to the human with a recommendation. If approved, proceed to Phase 4a.\n\nAfter all artifacts are reviewed and approved, update the plan with the actual artifact paths.\n\n**Gate -- proceed when:**\n- [ ] proposal.md human-approved\n- [ ] All specs/<capability>/spec.md files human-approved\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQBCWACR9J0EPX5QHHR9N7KA","ts":"2026-04-29T01:15:48Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' skills/mission/templates/design-template.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Design Template\n\nUse this template for every `design.md`. Include all five mandatory sections below.\n\n## Architecture Decision\n\nFor greenfield missions or missions with explicit architecture changes, evaluate 2-3 architecture candidates. Score each candidate on every dimension from 0-3, then recommend one candidate.\n\nFor existing codebase missions without architecture changes, document the current architecture and explain how the planned changes fit. Scored candidates are not required.\n\n| Candidate | Separation of concerns | Testability | Task independence | Module depth | Total | Recommendation |\n|-----------|------------------------|-------------|-------------------|--------------|-------|----------------|\n| Candidate A | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-12 | Recommended / Not recommended |\n| Candidate B | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-12 | Recommended / Not recommended |\n\n### Scoring Rubric\n\n#### Separation of Concerns\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Each responsibility maps to exactly one unit. No unit mixes domain logic with transport, storage, or configuration. |\n| 2 | Mostly isolated but 1-2 units mix concerns pragmatically. |\n| 1 | Several units mix concerns. Domain logic tangled with transport or storage in more than one place. |\n| 0 | No clear separation. Business logic, I/O, and configuration interleaved throughout. |\n\n#### Testability\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Each module testable in isolation with swappable dependencies via interfaces or dependency injection. |\n| 2 | Most modules testable in isolation. One or two require a real external dependency. |\n| 1 | Tests require significant setup or multiple running services. Modules tightly coupled to dependencies. |\n| 0 | No practical way to test modules in isolation. Testing requires the full system running. |\n\n#### Task Independence\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Separately buildable or deployable units. A task can modify one unit without touching others. |\n| 2 | Explicit interface boundaries within a single build unit with enforced contracts. |\n| 1 | Directory/namespace separation only. No enforcement beyond developer discipline. |\n| 0 | Flat structure. No meaningful boundaries. Every task touches shared state. |\n\n#### Module Depth\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Modules expose small public API relative to implementation. Callers don't need to understand internals. |\n| 2 | Reasonable interface-to-implementation ratio. One or two modules expose more surface than they hide. |\n| 1 | Several modules are thin wrappers. Public API nearly as large as implementation. |\n| 0 | No meaningful abstraction. Public surface and implementation effectively the same. |\n\nAfter the table, include a short rationale for the recommended architecture. If candidates tie on total score, present the tied candidates and their tradeoffs for human selection.\n\n## Module Inventory\n\nList each planned module or major component. Use this table format:\n\n| Module name | Interface width | What it hides | Why this boundary |\n|-------------|-----------------|---------------|-------------------|\n| `<module>` | Narrow / moderate / broad, with the public entry points named | Implementation details callers do not need to know | Reason this responsibility belongs behind this boundary |\n\n## Type Catalog\n\nList domain types only. Type names must come from `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` when the term exists there. Do not include field-level detail.\n\n| Type name | Purpose | Owning module |\n|-----------|---------|---------------|\n| `<type>` | One-line purpose | `<module>` |\n\n## Error Strategy\n\nDefine:\n- Error type hierarchy\n- Propagation rules\n- Which layer throws each error category\n- Which layer catches and translates each error category\n\nKeep this at the design level. Do not list every possible error instance unless the specs require it.\n\n## Integration Points\n\nDescribe how modules call each other. Use this table format:\n\n| Caller | Callee | Method/interface | Direction |\n|--------|--------|------------------|-----------|\n| `<module>` | `<module>` | Function, command, event, adapter, or interface name | Inbound, outbound, or internal |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQBCWACR9J0EPX5QHHR9N7KA","ts":"2026-04-29T01:15:48Z","tool":"command_execution"}
